+# Privacy by Design — The Daily Grind
+
+How the website protects visitors' privacy, and how to keep it that way.
+
+## Principles
+
+| Principle | Implementation |
+|---|---|
+| **Private by default** | No analytics, advertising or tracking. Optional categories (analytics, marketing) start **off** and only turn on after an explicit opt-in. |
+| **Respect browser signals** | `navigator.globalPrivacyControl` (and legacy Do Not Track) force opt-out and lock the toggles. Declared in `/.well-known/gpc.json`. |
+| **Data minimization** | The only client-side data stored is `localStorage.dg_privacy`: `{v, date (day precision), choices}`. No cookies, no IDs. It expires after 180 days. Tracking query params (`utm_*`, `gclid`, `fbclid`, …) are removed from the URL on load. |
+| **No silent third parties** | Strict CSP (`default-src 'none'`, no inline code). Every external origin must be listed in `privacy/third-parties.json` and allowed in the CSP. Third-party scripts can only be added as consent-gated placeholders. |
+| **Secure data handling** | Logout is a `POST` (it can't be triggered cross-site with a link/image or by prefetch). Logout clears `sessionStorage` and `dg_session_*` keys, and the response sends `Clear-Site-Data`. Account pages are `Cache-Control: no-store`. Pages restored from the back/forward cache are reloaded. |
+| **Minimal leakage** | `Referrer-Policy: same-origin`, external links get `noopener noreferrer`, and a `Permissions-Policy` turns off camera, microphone, geolocation, Topics and other features. |
+
+## Files
+
+- `index.html` — CSP and referrer `<meta>` fallbacks, POST logout, "Your Privacy Choices" dialog.
+- `assets/js/privacy.js` — consent manager, GPC handling, URL param stripping, link hardening, logout cleanup.
+- `assets/css/styles.css` — all styles (external so the CSP can forbid inline styles).
+- `_headers` — HTTP security headers (Netlify/Cloudflare Pages format; port them to your host if it differs).
+- `privacy/third-parties.json` — audited inventory of third-party origins.
+- `scripts/privacy-audit.mjs` — `npm run audit:privacy`; run it in CI.
+
+## Adding a third party (e.g. analytics)
+
+1. Do a privacy review: what data it receives, whether it sets cookies, where data is stored, retention, DPA.
+2. Add an entry to `privacy/third-parties.json` with a non-`necessary` category and the CSP directives it needs.
+3. Add the origin to the CSP in **both** `_headers` and the `<meta>` in `index.html`.
+4. Include it only as a gated placeholder:
+   ```html
+   <script type="text/plain" data-consent="analytics" data-src="https://vendor.example/sdk.js"
+           integrity="sha384-..."></script>
+   ```
+   The consent dialog then appears automatically for visitors who haven't chosen yet (unless GPC is on).
+5. Run `npm run audit:privacy`.
+
+## Known exception
+
+The hero photo comes from `images.unsplash.com`. That host sees the visitor's IP and User-Agent, but no Referer or cookies. **Recommended:** download the image to `assets/img/hero.jpg`, point `.hero` at it, and remove the Unsplash entry from the CSP and the inventory.
+
+## Server-side responsibilities (outside this repo)
+
+- Protect `POST /logout` and every other state-changing endpoint with CSRF tokens / `SameSite=Lax` (or `Strict`) cookies.
+- Set session cookies with `Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax` and the `__Host-` prefix.
+- Keep retention limits for orders, rewards and logs, and drop or truncate IP addresses in access logs.
